      },
      "text": "### Lucien Febvre (1878-1956)"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-ANNALES-SCHOOL.md#012",
      "path": [
        "01-ANNALES-SCHOOL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:01-annales-school, git-history:social-history:01-annales-school]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/01-ANNALES-SCHOOL.md",
        "concepts": "[annales, school]",
        "current_path": "social-history/01-ANNALES-SCHOOL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:annales-school",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[annales, school]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "The Annales School and Total History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n  KEY WORKS:\n  The Problem of Unbelief in the Sixteenth Century (1942):\n  Was Rabelais an atheist? Febvre's answer: you could not be an atheist\n  in the 16th century because the mental equipment (outillage mental)\n  for atheism did not yet exist."
    },
    {
      "id": "01-ANNALES-SCHOOL.md#013",
      "path": [
        "01-ANNALES-SCHOOL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:01-annales-school, git-history:social-history:01-annales-school]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/01-ANNALES-SCHOOL.md",
        "concepts": "[annales, school]",
        "current_path": "social-history/01-ANNALES-SCHOOL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:annales-school",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[annales, school]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "The Annales School and Total History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "MENTAL TOOLS (outillage mental):\n  Each epoch has its own categories, concepts, and mental habits.\n  You cannot think outside the available mental toolkit of your time.\n  This prefigures Kuhn's paradigms and Foucault's epistemes."
    },
    {
      "id": "01-ANNALES-SCHOOL.md#014",
      "path": [
        "01-ANNALES-SCHOOL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:01-annales-school, git-history:social-history:01-annales-school]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/01-ANNALES-SCHOOL.md",
        "concepts": "[annales, school]",
        "current_path": "social-history/01-ANNALES-SCHOOL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:annales-school",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[annales, school]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "The Annales School and Total History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "THE PROGRAM:\n  Febvre wanted history to engage with geography, economics, psychology,\n  sociology — an interdisciplinary \"total history.\"\n  Against \"narrow\" political and diplomatic history.\n  \"L'histoire totale\": capturing the full range of human experience."
    }
  ],
